3. Authoritative decision sequence
For Mainnet Core/Internal/External investment, the server must evaluate in this order:
Authenticate the Pi access token against the Pi Platform API and derive the UID from that verified response. Never trust a UID supplied by the browser.
Derive the network from the deployed gateway configuration. Do not allow request-body network to switch the server's target database or Pi API.
Load the authoritative project using its stable database ID/code and the expected network.
Confirm the exact allowed project type and registration source. No type fall-through to Core.
Require project.status = 'active' for every Mainnet investment/staking route.
Confirm investment is enabled for that project/activity and the correct published, effective staking term exists for the exact project and requested duration.
Confirm treasury/network/wallet and all required liquidity/readiness conditions for the applicable project type.
Verify the Pi PaymentDTO server-side: payment ID, U2A direction, expected network, exact sender UID, amount, recipient, payment status, project/action metadata, and accepted term/contract version.
Approve/completion-confirm via the Pi Platform API and re-read payment state after completion.
In one authoritative database transaction, settle the verified payment and create at most one stake. A failed gate must leave no stake and no partial financial ledger effect.
Return a success response only after the authoritative settlement RPC confirms success.
The browser may make the user experience clearer, but cannot override any of these gates.
4. Project-type boundaries
4.1 Core
Existing pathway reviewed:
Frontend: albukhr-app/js/staking.js
Project page: albukhr-app/js/project.js
API: albukhr-api/src/financial-gateway.js
Mainnet settlement: public.create_stake_from_completed_payment and public.settle_project_liquidity_payment
Existing source checks include Mainnet-only behavior, Core project identity, ACTIVE status, published staking terms, treasury, and idempotent payment/stake handling in the settlement functions. Preserve the Core contract; do not broaden the Core route to accept Internal/External projects.
4.2 Internal / Contributor Internal
Existing pathway reviewed:
Frontend: albukhr-app/js/internal-staking.js
API: albukhr-api/src/internal-investment-gateway.js
Mainnet settlement: public.create_internal_stake_from_completed_payment
Existing checks include project_type = internal, registration_source = contributor_internal, Mainnet network, ACTIVE status, treasury readiness, liquidity threshold, published term and owner/identity-specific protections in the applicable liquidity path. Preserve these checks and close sender UID validation gaps described in Section 7.
4.3 External Projects
The read-only repository review did not identify a dedicated External investment/staking API and settlement RPC equivalent to the Core and Internal pathways. The Marketplace engine checks that a project is active, but delegates to the Core staking engine. The Core gateway then rejects non-Core project types. This can reject the attempted investment server-side, but it may do so only after the user has started a Pi payment, creating a risk of an incomplete/pending payment.
Before External investment is enabled:
Add a project-type guard in the Marketplace/user flow before Pi.createPayment.
Create a dedicated External investment gateway and settlement contract, if investment in External Projects is part of the approved business model.
Bind external application, converted project ID, External readiness/activation record, treasury/custody model, published terms, and payment ID server-side.
Require Mainnet External project status active; approved applications or registry records must be rejected for Mainnet investment.
Revalidate External eligibility at both approval and completion, not just when the payment starts.
Never let an External payment write Core liquidity/stake tables unless an explicitly reviewed contract defines that relationship.
External activation must remain distinct from merely approving an external application.
5. Testnet-only testing pathway
The current Testnet UI inspected (test-albukhr-app/index.html and test-albukhr-app/project.html) explicitly says Investment/Staking are LOCKED / not enabled. The current Testnet API code reviewed includes a Testnet-only project-liquidity payment router, not an investor staking gateway. Therefore the desired APPROVED-project staking test exception is not implemented/available in the inspected Testnet UI today.
If authorized for future development, build a dedicated testing flow with these controls:
Environment must be testnet; Testnet Supabase Core must report network = testnet.
Use Pi SDK sandbox/Testnet configuration and the Testnet Pi Platform/Horizon endpoints only.
Project record must be loaded from Testnet public.projects; never look up a Mainnet project as a fallback.
For test investment only, accept project status approved or active according to this policy. Do not mutate Mainnet project status to achieve this.
Use a Testnet-specific payment route and test settlement function; do not call the Mainnet /api/pi-payment-approve, /api/pi-payment-complete, or Mainnet settlement RPCs.
Persist only Testnet payment/stake rows in the Testnet database. Ensure every query/write is scoped to network = 'testnet' even where the database is already dedicated to Testnet.
Label the UI and records as TESTNET / TEST ONLY — NO MAINNET VALUE.
Do not allow testing to call Mainnet treasury, production fee/settlement functions, or Mainnet payout paths.
A Testnet transaction may test the Pi Testnet blockchain but does not prove Mainnet incoming-multisig readiness, Mainnet treasury configuration, or production liquidity readiness.
Keep the Testnet investment gateway disabled until the test contract, auth/session checks, server verification, recovery and regression tests are approved.
6. dApp Launch Service and liquidity separation
The dApp Launch Service has its own request/quote/payment model and dedicated payment functions:
Mainnet API: albukhr-api/src/dapp-service-v2-gateway.js (gateway disabled by default unless DAPP_SERVICE_V2_ENABLED=true; Pi payments also require separate enablement).
Tables: dapp_service_requests, dapp_service_quotes, dapp_service_payments, dapp_service_audit_events.
Settlement/status functions include dapp_service_prepare_pi_payment, dapp_service_record_pi_approval, dapp_service_confirm_pi_payment, and dapp_service_fail_pi_payment.
Rules:
dApp service payments pay an accepted quote for the service. They must not create a stake or settle Core/Internal/External investment.
Payment status, request status, quote status and delivery status remain separate.
Keep the service gateway and payment channel disabled until independently reviewed and expressly enabled.
Keep existing Pi Studio Assistance and Developer-built dApp workflows separate from the albukhr_project project pathway; Core projects are excluded from the Build an App for My ALBUKHR Project pathway.
Project liquidity contributions update the applicable project-liquidity ledger and treasury only. Liquidity completion must not silently create an investor stake.
7. High-priority payment verification hardening
The Mainnet Core and Internal investment gateways contain an assertSender pattern that rejects a mismatch when a sender UID is present, but does not reject a missing sender UID. Before live U2A is enabled, require a non-empty server-returned Pi payment sender UID and exact equality with the UID derived from the verified bearer token.
Also require, using fields supported by the actual Pi PaymentDTO/API response:
U2A direction is exactly correct.
Payment network is explicitly verified as the expected network; missing/unknown network must fail closed unless the authoritative Pi API contract documents that field as legitimately absent and another server-side proof is used.
Recipient must be present and exactly equal the expected treasury/app wallet before approval and after completion.
Completion txid matches the verified Pi payment transaction; a client-supplied txid alone is never proof.
Payment status is valid for the requested transition; approved/completed retries must be idempotent and state-aware.
Payment metadata and accepted staking-term version match the persisted investment intent. Do not silently settle under a newer staking-term version than the one the user accepted.
A duplicate payment ID cannot be rebound to a different UID, project, network, amount, recipient, duration, quote or term version.
These are proposed requirements for future patches; this document does not change the code.
8. Acceptance and regression tests
All tests should use controlled fixtures and Testnet-only payment simulation until production approval. Test cases should include at least:
Mainnet status gates
Core + approved → API approval rejected; no successful stake.
Internal + approved → API approval rejected; no successful stake.
External + approved → rejected before Pi SDK launch and by any server route.
Core/Internal/External + draft, restricted, paused, suspended, closed, missing or unknown status → reject.
Each eligible project type + active but missing/expired terms → reject.
active project with wrong/missing treasury, inadequate liquidity, mismatched project identity, or missing required readiness state → reject.
Change status from active to a blocking status between approval and completion → completion rejects and no stake is created.
Call the Mainnet Core route with an Internal/External project ID/code → reject; no cross-type fallback.
Network isolation
Testnet token/session against Mainnet API → reject.
Mainnet token against Testnet gateway → reject.
Testnet request body claiming network=mainnet → cannot change Testnet server configuration/data source.
Testnet payment ID or row provided to Mainnet settlement → reject.
Confirm all Testnet test stakes/payment records remain in Testnet Supabase only.
Pi payment verification and recovery
Missing sender UID → reject approval and completion.
Sender UID mismatches verified bearer UID → reject.
Missing/wrong payment direction, unexpected/missing network, wrong amount, wrong recipient, project metadata, duration or term version → reject.
Cancelled/failed/unapproved payment → must not create a stake.
Completion with wrong txid or transaction not verified by Pi → reject.
Repeat approval callback, repeat completion callback and two simultaneous completion requests → no duplicate stake or duplicate settlement ledger entry.
Pi payment is approved or transaction was submitted but HTTP/network response was lost → recover from authoritative Pi state; do not start a second payment blindly.
Incomplete payment callback must trigger a controlled recovery process, not only write to browser console.
Database failure after Pi completion → record recoverable state; user must not be told settlement succeeded unless settlement can be confirmed on retry.
Service separation
dApp service quote payment completes → dApp Service records update; no stake is created.
Liquidity payment completes → applicable liquidity/treasury ledger updates only; no investor stake is created by that path.
Pi dApp Launch Service payment channels remain disabled until separate approval.
External readiness approval does not itself turn on staking unless a separate authorized activation transition and dedicated External investment route both permit it.
Authorization
anon and authenticated cannot execute service-role-only settlement RPCs.
Invalid/expired Pi token, missing UID, and cross-user payment ID cannot settle funds for another user.
No error/recovery path bypasses canonical auth, network checks, server-side status checks or transaction audit.
9. Findings from the inspected code/database
Core UI and Core gateway enforce ACTIVE, and the Core settlement RPC rechecks project status/type/network.
Internal UI/gateway/settlement pathway also enforces ACTIVE, internal project type and contributor_internal registration source.
Mainnet Core/Internal settlement RPC execution privileges were observed as service-role-only; anon and authenticated cannot execute them directly.
The inspected External project workflow includes a separate activation readiness process, but a dedicated External investment/staking gateway and settlement function were not identified in this read-only review.
The inspected Testnet project UI says investment/staking are locked, and the Testnet API route inspected handles liquidity contributions rather than investor stake creation.
Mainnet project status counts at audit time: 5 Core approved, 2 Core draft, 1 Internal approved; no active or External project rows were present in the query result.
Testnet project status counts at audit time: 5 Core approved, 1 Internal approved; no active or External project rows were present in the query result.
Mainnet dApp Service table counts at audit time: 1 request, 0 quotes, 0 payments. This describes those tables only; it does not by itself establish any broader payment history.
10. Proposed implementation sequence
Phase A — No-write review: approve or amend this contract; capture exact production/Testnet branch and deployment SHAs; verify the complete Pi PaymentDTO schema and all routes that can create a stake.
Phase B — Patch proposal only: draft minimal additive patches and SQL migration proposals for (1) fail-closed sender/network/direction/recipient/term validation, (2) incomplete-payment recovery/idempotency, (3) pre-payment project-type/status guards, (4) dedicated External investment path if required, and (5) dedicated Testnet approved-project testing path. Do not apply these changes yet.
Phase C — Disposable Testnet QA: use Testnet-only project/payment fixtures; test all acceptance cases above, including retries, network failure, wrong identity and duplicate callbacks. Do not use Mainnet Pi or Mainnet Supabase for QA.
Phase D — Separate explicit go-live approval: only after test results, Mainnet Incoming Multisig Wallet approval/connection, treasury mapping, Mainnet verification and deployment plan are reviewed. No production payment enablement occurs under this proposal alone.
